```

Check whether production incidents can actually be diagnosed.

---

# 40. LOGGING HYGIENE

Look for:

- plaintext secrets
- password logs
- full token logs
- sensitive payload logging
- excessive debug logs
- inconsistent formats
- missing correlation ID
- unbounded logs

Prefer shared conventions where useful.

---

# 41. PERFORMANCE & RESOURCE AUDIT

Do not optimize prematurely.

Look for:

- slow queries
- N+1 queries
- huge payloads
- blocking loops
- memory leaks
- connection exhaustion
- unnecessary serial calls
- inefficient builds
- excessive container size
- startup delay

Classify:

```text
MEASURED
LIKELY
SPECULATIVE
```

Only prioritize measured or strong likely issues.

---

# 42. COST AUDIT

Where relevant evaluate:

- compute
- storage
- egress
- managed DB
- cache
- queue
- monitoring
- AI inference
- CDN
- object storage
- duplicate services
- idle environments

Classify:

```text
SAVE NOW
OPTIMIZE LATER
NO ACTION
```

---

# 43. REPOSITORY HYGIENE AUDIT

Audit the cleanliness and maintainability of every repository.

Look for:

- dead code
- unused files
- unused directories
- abandoned experiments
- duplicate implementation
- duplicate configuration
- unused dependencies
- stale dependencies
- generated artifacts accidentally committed
- binaries committed unnecessarily
- build artifacts
- temporary files
- backup files
- old migration artifacts
- commented-out code
- obsolete scripts
- unused Docker files
- outdated Compose files
- deprecated configs
- obsolete CI workflows
- abandoned feature flags
- stale environment variables
- unused secrets references
- stale branches
- merged branches that can be removed
- abandoned MR/PR
- duplicate documentation
- outdated documentation
- inconsistent naming
- inconsistent directory structure
- misplaced files
- multiple competing implementations
- old proof-of-concept code
- test/debug files leaking into production paths

Never delete anything merely because its name looks suspicious.

Confirm usage first.

